  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 255 PM CDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nBy early Thursday morning, impressive synoptic lift and some \ntransient but present isentropic ascent arrives in the central \nPlains to further support continued moderate to heavy rain across \nthat expansive area. Locally, this heavier rain continues to be \nfocused across northern Missouri and west-central Illinois, \nwith increasing signals in the ensemble guidance for a sharper \ngradient with southward extent. Probabilities for exceeding 2 \ninches of rain in this axis across northern Missouri continue to\nincrease according to the NBM and LREF, with localized \nprobability-matched mean output from the HREF and REFS beginning\nto paint concerning localized amounts of 4-6 inches. Most of \nthis higher-end potential will likely miss us to the north \nthrough Thursday, but a swath of up to 3 inches appears more \nlikely to clip northeastern Missouri by late Thursday. We will \nneed to monitor this potential, but as it stands the duration of\nthe rainfall likely precludes a flash flood threat. The current\nFlood Watch in northwestern Missouri was not extended further \neast. \n\nBy Thursday night, the wave aloft becomes weaker and more \nprogressive. As a result, all available guidance begins to shift the \nwaves of rain south and east. That said, as the wave weakens, the \nsynoptic support that maintained a favorable heavy rain environment \nbecomes more muted. Moisture transport from the more tropical \nairmass further south and west also weakens, but ambient \nprecipitable water values remain quite high until the front clears \nthe region. What this spells for those closer to St. Louis and areas \nsouth/east is decreasing widespread rainfall amounts with scattered \ninstances of higher totals where slightly deeper convection can \noccur. While high-end potential evident in cluster analyses of the \nLREF does still highlight a secondary corridor of heavier rainfall \nalong I-44 into the St. Louis metropolitan area, most guidance \nfavors a quicker solution with lower amounts. The front largely \nclears the region by late Friday, with some low threat for weak \nanafrontal showers lingering into Saturday. By that point, a \nreinforcing wave looks likely to not only force the front further \nsouth, but also usher in cooler, drier air for the weekend and early \nnext week.\n\nThe increasing potential for minor to moderate river flooding on the \nMississippi and Missouri Rivers and their tributaries becomes a more \nconcrete concern 4-7 days following this rainfall despite the \nheaviest rain falling to our north. Some output from the Hydrologic \nEnsemble Forecast System suggests low (15-30%) chances for major \nflooding in some spots on the mainstems north and west of St. Louis \nin a reasonable worst-case scenario. This worst-case would come to \nfruition only if the front were slower and synoptic forcing/lift \nwere stronger, adding more rain to the system upstream. No River \nFlood Warnings are in effect for our region, but close attention is \nbeing paid to the threat as it materializes in the coming days.",
